(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-clinical-reasoning-quiz"]={"title":"Clinical Reasoning and Problem Solving — Quiz 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"When building a differential for shortness of breath, the lecture's first recommendation is to consider what?","choices":["Which treatment is most likely to be tolerated","The signs and symptoms and which organ or system may be involved","The base rate of every disease in the population","Whether the patient is stable enough for the naturalistic approach"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's worked example asks whether shortness of breath is cardiac, pulmonary or haematologic — organ and system first. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed among the lecture's suggestions for good decision-making?","choices":["Decide quickly to avoid delaying care","Remember you are often wrong, and consider the immediate implications","Gather every available piece of data before deciding","Rely on the first diagnosis that appears to fit the presenting clinical picture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This appears alongside slowing down, weighing base rates, considering what data is truly relevant, and seeking alternatives. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"In VINDICATE, the letter E stands for which category?","choices":["Environmental exposure","Embolic","Eosinophilic","Endocrine (metabolic)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. E closes the mnemonic as Endocrine, with metabolic given in parentheses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"When the diagnosis carries some uncertainty, what balance must the decision to treat take into account?","choices":["The benefit of screening the wider population against the financial cost of confirmatory testing for each positive result","The clinician's experience against published guidelines","The benefit of treating a sick person against the risk of erroneously treating a well person or one with a different disorder","The speed of the test against its accuracy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This is the lecture's statement of the risk-versus-benefit balance under diagnostic uncertainty. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does the lecture give as the purpose of screening testing?","choices":["To identify the likelihood of occult disease","To complement the history and physical examination","To confirm a diagnosis already strongly suspected","To monitor a patient's response to treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening testing is defined as identifying the likelihood of occult disease — disease not yet apparent. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What do the Five As stand for in the lecture's counselling strategies?","choices":["Assess, adhere, arrange, advise, and act","Ask, advise, assess, assist, and arrange","Ask, assess, agree, assist, and arrange","Advise, appraise, assess, assist, and arrange"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. These are the Five As as listed. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which caution does the lecture attach to the use of clinical guidelines?","choices":["They should be avoided because they are rarely cost-effective","They should be used only once evidence-based medicine has failed to answer the question","They should be applied to patients with similar characteristics to those studied","They should replace the standard of care in complex cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guidelines are described as straightforward and easy to use but should be applied to patients with similar characteristics. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What does the lecture advise regarding where a patient sits on the continuum of behaviour change?","choices":["Apply the same standardised intervention regardless of where the patient sits on that continuum","Delay counselling until the patient is ready to change","Identify where they are and tailor interventions to their readiness and self-efficacy","Refer every patient for specialist behavioural therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture advises identifying the patient's position on the continuum and tailoring interventions to their readiness and self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts an intuitive system of thinking with what alternative?","choices":["A system that avoids all use of probability","A system driven entirely by patient preference","A more deliberate and controlled thought process using logic and probabilities","A system that applies only to unstable patients presenting to emergency medicine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs quick, automatic thinking against a deliberate, controlled process that uses logic and probabilities. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which clinical principle from the lecture most directly reflects the influence of how common a condition is?","choices":["If what you are doing is working, keep on doing it","If you do not know what to do, do not do anything","Follow responses to interventions","When you hear hoof beats think of horses, not zebras"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This principle, alongside \"common things occur commonly,\" is the lecture's statement that frequency should weight your differential. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient understands their diagnosis and wants treatment but repeatedly misses doses because of the cost of the medication. Which barrier does this represent?","choices":["Cost and affordability of medications or services","Low health literacy or lack of understanding","Cultural or religious beliefs about treatment","Poor communication or follow-up by the care team"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cost and affordability is listed first among the barriers to treatment and medication adherence. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"In the confusion example, which cause does the lecture place under Iatrogenic/intoxication?","choices":["Hypoglycaemia","Narcotics, and alcohol intoxication or withdrawal","Central nervous system lupus and neurosarcoidosis","Primary brain tumour"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists narcotics and alcohol intoxication or withdrawal as the iatrogenic and intoxication causes of confusion. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"The alternative mnemonic \"Tom G. Prince, MD, Psychiatrist, General Hospital\" is used for what purpose?","choices":["To sequence the six steps of the clinical reasoning process","To structure the counselling of a patient about adherence","As an alternative organ-system checklist when building a differential","To decide whether the benefit of treatment outweighs its risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is offered alongside VINDICATE as a way to run through systems — toxin/trauma, oncologic, musculoskeletal, gastrointestinal, pulmonary, renal, infectious, neurologic, cardiovascular, endocrine, metabolic/genetic, dermatologic, psychiatric, genitourinary, haematologic. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which statement about what sensitivity does NOT tell you is accurate?","choices":["Sensitivity does not address false-positives","Sensitivity does not address false-negatives","Sensitivity does not apply to screening tests","Sensitivity does not apply to people who have the disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture footnotes this directly — sensitivity speaks to false-negatives only, and says nothing about false-positive results. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which patient circumstances does the lecture list as suiting the naturalistic approach?","choices":["Unstable patients and atypical presentations","Stable patients with classic presentations","Patients enrolled in a screening programme","Patients who have already completed treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are listed, along with ruling out the worst-case scenario and following responses to interventions. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which pairing correctly separates pretest from posttest probability?","choices":["Pretest uses sensitivity and specificity; posttest uses risk factors","Both are determined solely by how common the disease is","Pretest is estimated from signs, symptoms, history and frequency; posttest additionally reflects the test result","Pretest probability applies to screening programmes only, while posttest probability applies to diagnostic testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. This matches the lecture's two definitions and their listed inputs. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The naturalistic (event-driven) approach is characterised by which practice?","choices":["Withholding all treatment until a definitive diagnosis is confirmed","Applying a published if-then guideline to a stable patient","Treating signs and symptoms before a definitive diagnosis is reached","Formulating a clinical question and reviewing the literature"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines the naturalistic, event-driven approach as treating signs and symptoms before a definitive diagnosis. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which pair of barriers to adherence does the lecture list?","choices":["Excess follow-up appointments and over-explanation of the possible side effects of treatment","Cultural or religious beliefs about treatment, and fear of side effects or mistrust of providers","High self-efficacy and strong family support","Short travel distance and abundant appointment availability"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both appear on the lecture's list of adherence barriers. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Posttest probability is the likelihood of the condition at which point, and depends on what?","choices":["Before the result is known; depends on how common the condition is","After the result is known; depends on sensitivity and specificity","After the result is known; depends only on the patient's risk factors","Before treatment is started; depends on the patient's ability to tolerate it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines posttest probability as the likelihood after the test result is known, and states it depends on sensitivity and specificity. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which two limitations of evidence-based medicine does the lecture identify?","choices":["It is time consuming, and many clinical questions have no relevant studies in the literature","It is inexpensive, and it applies only to rare diseases","It cannot be combined with clinical guidelines, and it disregards patient values entirely","It requires quantitative techniques, and it excludes the physical examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the two limitations given. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the value of treatment versus no treatment?","choices":["As a linear function of the probability of disease","As independent of the probability of disease","As determined solely by the cost of the treatment","As fixed once a working diagnosis has been reached"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states the value of treatment versus no treatment is a linear function of the probability of disease. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the middle of the clinical reasoning process?","choices":["Generate the hypotheses first, then organise and interpret the clinical information gathered from the patient","Establish a working diagnosis, then generate hypotheses","Organise and interpret information, synthesise and develop the problem representation, generate hypotheses","Plan treatment strategy, then test hypotheses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. These are Steps 2, 3 and 4 in order. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which set of inputs does the lecture list as informing pretest probability?","choices":["Sensitivity, specificity, and the test result","Signs and symptoms, history and risk factors, and how common the condition is in the population","Cost, availability, and expected turnaround time of the test in the local laboratory setting","The patient's readiness to change and self-efficacy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly these three inputs under pretest probability. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Confirmatory (supplemental) testing for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is highly specific in order to accomplish what?","choices":["Detect as many infected individuals as possible","Shorten the time needed to obtain a result","Identify the virus before antibodies have developed","Minimise false-positive diagnoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states confirmatory supplemental testing is highly specific, minimising false-positive diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In the six-step clinical reasoning process, developing the problem representation occurs at which point?","choices":["Before any patient information is gathered","When clinical information is synthesised, at Step 3","Only after a working diagnosis is established","As the final step, after the treatment plan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Step 3 is to synthesise clinical information and develop the problem representation, between organising the information and generating hypotheses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"In the hypothetico-deductive sequence, what is the purpose of the hypothesis evaluation stage?","choices":["Gathering data to confirm or exclude the diagnostic hypotheses","Listing every potential diagnosis triggered by patient cues","Adding new hypotheses prompted by the data gathered","Monitoring the patient's response to management"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Evaluation is defined as data gathering to confirm or exclude the hypotheses already generated. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"In the FRAMES counselling strategy, what does the letter M represent?","choices":["Motivation of the clinician","Medication review","Monitoring of behaviour change","Menu of options"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. FRAMES is feedback about personal risk, responsibility of the patient, advice to change, menu of options, empathetic style, and promote self-efficacy. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How does the lecture characterise behavioural counselling?","choices":["As a task that is best delegated to non-clinical support staff within the practice","As unnecessary when a guideline exists","As effective only for tobacco cessation","As one of the most important skills for helping patients make behavioural changes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states changing behaviours is difficult and that behavioural counselling is one of the most important skills for supporting it. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Why does the lecture consider a few false-positives acceptable when screening for human immunodeficiency virus (HIV)?","choices":["Because false-positive results simply do not occur with tests that have high sensitivity for the condition","Because the screening test is also the confirmatory test","Because the condition is too rare for false-positives to matter","Because they can be corrected by later confirmatory testing, whereas a missed case may unknowingly infect others"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture weighs a correctable false-positive against failing to identify someone who might unknowingly infect others. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"In which setting does the lecture say the naturalistic approach is mostly used?","choices":["Outpatient preventive care","Elective surgical planning","Long-term chronic disease management","Emergency medicine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states it is used mostly in emergency medicine, for unstable patients and atypical presentations. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 24"}]};
